First half 18,740. Second half 93,220. Total 111,960. **Ratio 4.97.**

**That ratio is Corw's five and it is load-bearing in B1.CH22.** Worth flagging for anyone checking the chapter: the five is only derivable with the win-count climb included. The per-win rates alone, 20 into 120 into 240, give about 2.7. A reader who does the arithmetic off what Corw actually says out loud will not reach five.

## 4. Academy and military income

| Year | Days a week | Per win, year end | Year income | Cumulative | Income per month at year start |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 1 | 240 | 111,960 | 111,960 | 340 |
| 2 | 2 | 480 | 732,600 | 844,560 | 42,900 |
| 3 | 3 | 720 | 1,793,400 | 2,637,960 | 122,500 |
| 4 | 3 | 960 | 2,499,000 | 5,136,960 | 181,300 |
| 5 | 3 | 1,200 | 3,204,600 | 8,341,560 | 240,100 |
| 6 | 3 | 1,440 | 3,910,200 | 12,251,760 | 298,900 |
| 7 | 3 | 1,680 | 4,615,800 | 16,867,560 | 357,700 |

**Year 1 is untouched by the Saturday ruling,** which is the whole reason for putting Saturday at Year 2 rather than Year 1. Every delivered figure, Ch.11's 2,140, Ch.21's 14,640, Corw's 16,500 and 406, and B1.CH22's entire middle-board scene, stands exactly as written.

**Year 2 rises to 732,600, not 754,800.** Doubling the Year 2 total is the wrong arithmetic and I did it that way once before writing this table. Class activity and graded baseline don't scale with combat days, only the combat component does.

The climb is steepest at Years 1 to 2 and 2 to 3, which is now a schedule fact rather than a curve: the number of combat days a week goes one, two, three, and the per-win value keeps climbing underneath it. The Year 3 step coincides with the specialisation gate opening, which is convenient rather than accidental. The year a cadet can finally buy a weapon is the year the income arrives to buy one with.

**Military,** at eight missions a month and two kills a mission.

| Service month | Income per month |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 112,000 |
| 6 | 1,312,000 |
| 12 | 2,752,000 |
| 24 | 5,632,000 |
| 36 | 8,512,000 |
| 36, unit-commander tier | 25,536,000 |

First year of service alone, 17,184,000, more than the entire seven-year academy.

## 5. The path boards, repriced against the gate

**The rule.** A path grade is priced so that it takes a real bite out of what a cadet has when that grade realistically lands, measured against the gate rather than against a grade-year table. The first purchase after specialising should cost about half of everything the cadet has ever earned. G stays where it is, since G is issued gear priced for comparison.

| Grade | Band | Lands | Against |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| G | 180 – 220 | Issued, Y1 | Not really bought |
| F | 360,000 – 430,000 | Y3, at specialisation | 844,560 banked the day the gate opens |
| E | 1,400,000 – 1,700,000 | Late Y3 | Y3 income 1,793,400 |
| D | 3,700,000 – 4,400,000 | Y4-5 to afford, later to actually buy | Cumulative 8,341,560 by end Y5 |
| C | 15,000,000 | Military era | First service year 17,184,000 |
| B | 60,000,000 | Mid service | Month-24 income 5,632,000 |
| A | 250,000,000 | Unit-commander standing | 25,536,000 a month at that tier |

**The genetic board does not move and cannot.** 130, 74,000 and 76,000,000 are on the page in B1.CH22. That leaves the open board sitting more than twenty times below the path boards at E, which is a deliberate asymmetry rather than an inconsistency, and it has a reason the prose must never state: the institution prices what it wants cadets to want. A weapon is visible and creates rank. The thing that goes in you doesn't show, so it's cheap, and two thousand cadets walk past it for five months without one of them looking up.

## 5a. Trav's locked ladder against the new prices

v1 §5 placed the Heavylance in Year 1 Q2 and the Twinlance in Year 2. Both are impossible under the gate and both contradict the locked chapter addresses. This is the defect the lost v2 caught and it is corrected here against those addresses, which are the authority.

| Purchase | Address | Cost | What he has, what it leaves |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Stocklance | Issued, Y1 | 200 | Issued to every cadet, priced for comparison only |
| Heavylance | B3.CH24 | 400,000 | Holding about 1,653,000 mid-Y3, leaves 1,253,000 |
| Twinlance | B3.CH43 | 1,600,000 | Holding about 2,062,000 late-Y3, leaves 462,000 |
| Pairblade | B6.CH13 | 4,000,000 | Clears it during Y5, buys in Y6 |
| Arcblade | Military era | 15,000,000 | Academy endpoint is the Pairblade, per WEAP |

**Two things this shape does that the old one didn't.**

He ends Year 3 nearly cleaned out. Two purchases in the year the gate opens, and the second one takes seventy-eight percent of everything he's holding. That is a year with a real cost attached rather than a year with two acquisitions in it.

The Pairblade delay is a character fact and not an economic one. He can afford it from partway through Year 5 and doesn't buy it until Year 6, because the swap is a philosophy shift he has to actually discover. The catalog always required that and the old pricing quietly undercut it by making the blades arrive exactly on schedule.

**His underinvestment is now worth naming.** Skipping Steadyburn is skipping 4,000,000. That is a visible, expensive choice by Year 5, not thrift.

## 6. Corrections this ruling forces elsewhere

- **Qiraki_Points_Shop_Catalog, regenerated as v3.** Single price column, path boards at the bands in §5, genetic board frozen at the page figures. Quickstep moves 150 to 130, which is on the page in B1.CH22 and is therefore not optional.

- **Qiraki_Combat_Curriculum_Reference, v5.** Its schedule line ran Saturday from Year 1 and is contradicted by every delivered Year 1 chapter. Corrected to one day, two days, three days across Years 1, 2 and 3 on.

- **Qiraki_Weapons_And_Progression, v4.** Its genetic/stimulant line calls the upgrades "role-specific tracks" available "years 1-3." Wrong on both counts under this ruling and against delivered prose: the board is universal, not role-specific, and it is open from week one. Its weapon-progression line reading "lance (Y1)" also needs to distinguish the issued Stocklance from the Heavylance purchase, which lands at specialisation.

- **A full kit is weapon plus power unit plus stimulant.** At E that is 3,274,000 against 2,637,960 cumulative by the end of Year 3, so a full E kit is just out of reach in the year a cadet specialises and lands early in Year 4. At D it is 8,340,000 against 8,341,560 cumulative by the end of Year 5, which means a cadet who saves everything is fully D-kitted almost to the point at the end of Year 5 and a cadet earning half as much has the weapon and nothing else. That gap is the snowball, and it is now visible in kit rather than asserted.

## 7. Still open

- **Baseline per graded item or per practice repetition.** v1 cited its companion audit's §6.1 for this. That audit is missing, so the question survives without its reasoning.

- **Does the mek matter-cost spend draw on the same points pool.** WEAP's cost split establishes that meks buy raw matter per unit while pilots buy the schematic. Nothing states whether that comes out of the same balance a pilot spends from, which matters the first time a chapter shows Reqa budgeting.
